From: Gavin McFall (Lighthouse) <gavin@nerdz.co.nz>
Subject: [PATCH] distributed: assume-online for mixed-content worker probes

ComfyUI-Distributed's UI probes worker status by direct HTTP fetch from the
browser. In cluster deployments (Lighthouse), the page is served over HTTPS
via the proxy (https://lighthouse.nerdz.cloud) but workers are LAN-only HTTP
(http://vengeancepc.internal:8188). The browser blocks ALL such probes as
mixed-content; the UI then thinks workers are offline forever and falls back
to master-only execution via /prompt — silently downgrading dispatch to a
CPU-master render path the user never asked for.

This patch makes probeWorker return assume-online when it detects the
mixed-content scenario (HTTPS page + HTTP worker URL). Justification:
- Server-side orchestration in /distributed/queue does a real probe at
  dispatch time and reports worker_count accurately
- If a worker is genuinely offline, master's dispatch returns worker_count=0
  and the UI surfaces that error inline
- The UI's display state is therefore informational; the source of truth is
  the server-side probe at queue time

Upstream-friendly: only changes behaviour in the mixed-content edge case
(HTTPS page + HTTP worker), which is a deployment configuration the plugin
upstream doesn't currently support. Same-origin/non-mixed-content probes
(e.g. master probing itself, single-machine deployments) are unaffected.

Lighthouse memory: comfyui-distributed-symmetric-install.md (sibling section
"Distributed-panel status is stale, not real-time")
Lighthouse PR context: Plan-1b cluster UX completion 2026-06-03

---
 web/apiClient.js | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/web/apiClient.js b/web/apiClient.js
--- a/web/apiClient.js
+++ b/web/apiClient.js
@@ -161,6 +161,19 @@

         async probeWorker(workerUrl, timeoutMs = TIMEOUTS.STATUS_CHECK, signal = null) {
             const normalizedWorkerUrl = normalizeWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
+            // LIGHTHOUSE PATCH: in cluster deployments the page is HTTPS but worker URLs
+            // are LAN-only HTTP — browser blocks the probe as mixed-content. The
+            // orchestration probe in /distributed/queue runs server-side and is the
+            // source of truth for dispatchability; this UI probe is informational only.
+            // Returning assume-online here makes the UI route requests through
+            // /distributed/queue (correct path); if a worker is genuinely offline,
+            // master's dispatch surfaces that as worker_count=0 in the response.
+            // Same-origin probes (master probing itself, single-machine deployments
+            // where worker URLs are HTTP and page is HTTP) are unaffected.
+            if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && window.location.protocol === 'https:'
+                && normalizedWorkerUrl.startsWith('http://')) {
+                return { ok: true, status: 200, queueRemaining: 0 };
+            }
             const controller = new AbortController();
             const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeoutMs);
             const effectiveSignal = signal
